From: hashtagbasit
Subject: scsi: ufs: qcom: keep the lane clock enables balanced

ufs_qcom_disable_lane_clks() returns early when the lane clocks are already
off, but ufs_qcom_enable_lane_clks() has no such check. Coming out of a
runtime or system suspend, ufs_qcom_resume() enables them and then
ufs_qcom_setup_clocks() enables them again for a link in hibern8, while the
next suspend disables them only once. Every cycle leaves one more prepare and
enable count on the UFS PHY symbol and AHB clocks.

On a Pocket FIT running from its internal UFS the counts were 43 a few
minutes after boot and 58 after one 2.4 minute s2idle. Those clocks never
turn off, so XO stays on and the SoC never gets past CPU cluster collapse:
the RPMh cxsd, aosd and ddr sleep counters stayed at 0 across the sleep.

Make the enable mirror the disable: do nothing when the clocks are already
on, so is_lane_clks_enabled tracks a single reference.

---
--- a/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c
@@ -356,6 +356,9 @@
 {
 	int err;

+	if (host->is_lane_clks_enabled)
+		return 0;
+
 	err = clk_bulk_prepare_enable(host->num_clks, host->clks);
 	if (err)
 		return err;
